osition)\n- Opponent re-raised all-in with 19,899 chips\n- I have 19,550 chips remaining\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nAQs is a premium starting hand in heads-up Texas Hold'em, roughly in the top 10% of all starting hands. It has good equity against most hands except premium pairs (AA, KK, QQ, potentially AK).\n\n**Opponent's Range Analysis:**\nAn all-in re-raise pre-flop is a very aggressive move that typically indicates:\n1. Premium pairs (AA, KK, QQ, JJ)\n2. Strong aces (AK, AQ)\n3. A bluff/semi-bluff with weaker hands trying to steal the pot\n\n**Pot Odds:**\n- Pot currently has: 400 (my bet) + 19,899 (opponent's all-in) = 20,299 chips\n- I need to call 19,499 more chips\n- Pot odds: 19,499 to win 20,299 (approximately 1:1)\n- I need roughly 49% equity to break even\n\n**Equity Considerations:**\n- Against AA: ~7% equity\n- Against KK: ~24% equity  \n- Against QQ: ~24% equity\n- Against AK: ~25-30% equity (if suited) or ~28-32% (if offsuit)\n- Against JJ or worse pairs: ~45-55% equity\n- Against weaker aces or random hands: 60%+ equity\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- This is only hand 9 of 20\n- I'm ahead by 19,300 chips cumulatively\n- Risking almost all my chips on one hand seems unnecessarily risky given my lead\n- Even with a premium hand like AQs, I likely don't have the required equity against a polarized all-in range\n\nReason:\nWhile AQs is a strong hand, facing an all-in reraise pre-flop is extremely dangerous. My opponent's range is likely heavily weighted toward premium hands (AA, KK, QQ, AK) against which I have poor equity (usually 25-35%). Given that I'm significantly ahead in the match and have 11 more hands to play, it's strategically unwise to risk my entire stack in a marginal spot. Folding preserves my chip lead and allows me to find better spots to accumulate chips.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
